\begin{abstract}
Action recognition models often rely on static spatial cues rather than motion: the background scene (background bias) and the actor's appearance (foreground bias). Foreground bias is comparatively underexplored, despite its implications for safety and fairness, and existing debiasing methods typically need extra supervision, optical flow, or decoupled encoders, and mostly target the background. We propose \approachname, a simple end-to-end adversarial training method with a single 3D encoder that needs no auxiliary labels. Each motion clip is paired with a static clip made by stacking one of its frames; the model is trained to classify the motion clip while \emph{not} confidently predicting the label on the static clip, using a negative cross-entropy (adversarial) term, an entropy-maximization term, and a gradient penalty on the static input. We also show that the widely used UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol leaks background through bounding-box masks, and release an improved protocol with tight segmentation masks. On HMDB51, \approachname{} reaches 53.22\% contrasted accuracy versus 40.28\% for the prior state of the art StillMix (+12.9 points) and 27.84\% for the baseline, and on the improved UCF101 protocol 26.23\% versus 24.40\% (StillMix) and 17.53\% (baseline). The debiased model also transfers to anomaly detection (UCF-Crime) and temporal action localization (THUMOS14).
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Video action recognition models are frequently right for the wrong reasons. Rather than modeling motion, they exploit spurious correlations with static spatial content, such as the scene in which an action usually occurs (\emph{background bias}: predicting ``Playing Soccer'' on any soccer field) or the actor's attire, skin tone and other appearance cues (\emph{foreground bias}) \citep{geirhos2020shortcut,choi2019can,li2023mitigating}. These shortcuts hurt robustness when a test scene is rare, and in applications such as surveillance, assisted living or autonomous systems they raise safety and fairness concerns. Background bias has received attention; foreground bias has received much less.

Existing remedies need extra supervision (scene or object labels, attribute classifiers) \citep{choi2019can,zhang2018mitigating}, rely on complex pipelines (optical flow, decoupled spatial and temporal encoders) \citep{sevilla2019integration}, or use augmentations that mainly address the background \citep{yun2020videomix,wang2021removing,ding2022motion,li2023mitigating}. Moreover, evaluation itself can be flawed: the UCF101 version of the SCUBA/SCUFO protocol \citep{li2023mitigating} builds actor masks from bounding boxes, so part of the original background remains visible and inflates the apparent robustness of models.

Our key insight is that if a model cannot confidently predict the action from a motionless clip, it must rely on motion. We therefore build an adversarial learning loop on a single 3D encoder: the model is trained to predict the correct label on a normal clip while confident label predictions on the corresponding static clip (one frame stacked over time) are discouraged. A pure adversarial term would let the model become confidently \emph{wrong} on static clips, so we additionally maximize the entropy of static predictions and stabilize training with a gradient penalty on the static input.

\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) \approachname, a simple end-to-end adversarial framework that requires no auxiliary attribute labels or critic networks and jointly discourages static-cue reliance, maximizes static uncertainty and stabilizes training. (ii) A study of foreground and background biases with state-of-the-art results on SCUBA/SCUFO, including +12.9 points of contrasted accuracy over the prior state of the art on HMDB51. (iii) Identification and correction of background leakage in the UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol via tight actor segmentation masks, which we release. (iv) Evidence of downstream benefits on anomaly detection and temporal action localization.

\section{Related Works}
\paragraph{Static bias in action recognition.} Video models are known to encode scene and appearance shortcuts \citep{choi2019can,li2018resound,li2019repair,huang2018makes,byvshev20223d,weinzaepfel2021mimetics}; shortcut learning is a broader phenomenon in deep networks \citep{geirhos2020shortcut,geirhos2018imagenet}. Scene-debiasing methods include adversarial scene-prediction heads with scene labels (SDN) \citep{choi2019can}, background erasing by adding backgrounds (BE) \citep{wang2021removing}, foreground--background contrastive objectives \citep{ding2022motion,ding2022dual}, open-set scene debiasing \citep{zhai2023soar}, and representation-bias benchmarks and algorithms \citep{duan2022mitigating}. Related are augmentations such as Mixup \citep{zhang2017mixup}, VideoMix \citep{yun2020videomix}, ActorCutMix \citep{zou2023learning}, FAME \citep{ding2022motion}, and StillMix \citep{li2023mitigating}, which also introduced the SCUBA/SCUFO protocol to measure background and foreground (static) bias. Other approaches use optical flow or separate motion streams \citep{sun2018optical,sevilla2019integration}.

\paragraph{Adversarial debiasing.} Adversarial training to remove protected or unwanted attributes from representations \citep{goodfellow2014generative,zhang2018mitigating,xie2017controllable,beutel2017data,elazar2018adversarial,bahng2020learningrebias} normally relies on attribute labels and an auxiliary critic. \approachname{} instead uses the model's own prediction on a static version of the input as the adversary, with no additional labels or networks. Bias and privacy in vision more broadly are discussed in \citet{ntoutsi2020bias,buolamwini2018gender,gustafson2023facet}.

\section{Method}
\subsection{Overview}
\approachname{} pairs each motion clip with a matched static clip built from a single frame and processes both with the same encoder $F$ (Figure~\ref{fig:arch}). Training combines supervised cross-entropy on motion clips, an adversarial negative cross-entropy on static clips, entropy maximization on static predictions and a gradient penalty on static inputs.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/main_architecture.png}
\caption{Overview of \approachname. Given a video clip $\bar{\mathbf{x}}$, we sample a frame and stack it to create a static clip. Both clips go through the same encoder.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Static adversarial loss}
Given a clip $x^{(i)}_t$ with label $y^{(i)}$, we sample a frame at time $\bar t$ and stack it $F$ times to form a static clip $\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t}$. With the same encoder, $p^{(i)}_t=F(x^{(i)}_t)$ and $\bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t}=F(\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t})$. The supervised loss on the motion clip is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{CE}=-\sum_{c=1}^{N_C} y^{(i)}_c\log p^{(i)}_c ,
\end{equation}
and the adversarial loss on the static clip maximizes cross-entropy (implemented by gradient reversal):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{adv}=-\mathcal{L}_{CE}\big(\bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t},y^{(i)}\big).
\end{equation}
This pushes the model not to align static predictions with the label, forcing reliance on motion.

\subsection{Entropy maximization on static clips}
Without a constraint, the model can learn to be confidently wrong on static inputs, which is itself a (inverted) static shortcut. We therefore maximize the entropy of $\bar p$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{ent}=\sum_c \bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t,c}\log \bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t,c}.
\end{equation}
Minimizing $\mathcal{L}_{ent}$ maximizes the entropy $H(p)=-\sum p\log p$ and encourages uniform predictions on static clips.

\subsection{Gradient penalty on static inputs}
Adversarial signals can make training unstable. We regularize the sensitivity of the model to static inputs:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{gp}=\big\lVert\nabla_{\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t}}F(\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t})\big\rVert_2 .
\end{equation}

\subsection{Combined objective}
The loss for sample $i$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}=\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{CE}+\omega_{adv}\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{adv}+\omega_{ent}\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{ent}+\omega_{gp}\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{gp}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Improved UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol}
SCUBA replaces the background of test videos with other scenes while preserving the actor (to measure background bias), and SCUFO stacks a single SCUBA frame to produce a static foreground-only clip (to measure foreground bias) \citep{li2023mitigating}. The original UCF101 protocol derived actor masks from THUMOS-14 bounding boxes \citep{THUMOS14}, which let background pixels around the actor leak into the SCUBA clips. We obtain tight actor masks with SAMTrack \citep{cheng2023segment}, built on SAM, AOT and DeAOT \citep{kirillov2023segment,yang2021aot,yang2022deaot}, initialized from the THUMOS-14 boxes. Masks are generated automatically and then manually checked for every test video to remove residual background around actors. Backgrounds are replaced as in prior work with Places365 test images \citep{zhou2017places}, VQGAN-CLIP generated images \citep{crowson2022vqgan}, or sinusoidal stripe patterns. Splits and class counts are inherited from the prior design; only mask quality changes. Figure~\ref{fig:scuba} contrasts the two protocols.

