\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Setup}
\paragraph{Evaluation.} We evaluate on HMDB51 \citep{kuehne2011hmdb} and UCF101 \citep{soomro2012ucf101} (IID, SCUBA, SCUFO, Confl-FG) and on the rare-scene ARAS benchmark. IID is standard Top-1 accuracy. Avg SCUBA is Top-1 on background-replaced clips (higher is better, less background bias); Avg SCUFO is Top-1 on static foreground-only clips (lower is better, less foreground/static bias); Confl-FG inserts a conflicting foreground on sinusoid SCUBA. \emph{Contrasted accuracy} counts a prediction correct only if the model is correct on a SCUBA clip and incorrect on its SCUFO counterpart, capturing simultaneous background and foreground debiasing.

\paragraph{Implementation.} The backbone is Video Swin-T \citep{liu2022video} pretrained on Kinetics-400 \citep{carreira2017quo}. Clips have 32 frames with stride 2 at $224\times224$, with random resized crop and horizontal flip. We use AdamW \citep{loshchilov2017decoupled} ($\beta_1=0.9$, $\beta_2=0.999$, weight decay 0.01), base learning rate $10^{-4}$ at batch size 64, 5 warmup epochs and a cosine schedule. Baselines are Mixup, VideoMix, SDN, BE, ActorCutMix, FAME and StillMix.

\subsection{Main results}
\paragraph{HMDB51.} Table~\ref{tab:hmdb_scuba_small} shows that \approachname{} raises contrasted accuracy from 27.84\% (no debiasing) to 53.22\%, i.e.\ +25.4 points, and +12.9 points over the strongest prior method StillMix (40.28\%). Avg SCUBA improves to 53.53\% (StillMix 51.81\%), Avg SCUFO drops to 1.50\% (StillMix 13.39\%; baseline 20.46\%) and Confl-FG rises to 48.13\% (StillMix 47.38\%). Combining \approachname{} with StillMix augmentation gives 53.68\% contrasted accuracy. These gains come at a cost in IID accuracy (72.81\% versus 73.92\% for the baseline and 74.82\% for StillMix). Most earlier methods barely change foreground bias (Avg SCUFO between 19.96\% and 26.92\%), whereas \approachname{} nearly removes the ability to classify from static foreground.

