\subsection{Ablations}
\paragraph{Loss components.} Table~\ref{tab:abl_framework} ablates the loss terms on HMDB51. Using $\mathcal{L}_{adv}$ alone collapses to a confidently wrong solution on static clips (Avg SCUFO $0.00$) with unstable learning and 42.29\% contrasted accuracy. $\mathcal{L}_{ent}$ alone reduces static reliance only modestly (44.30\%), less effectively than $\mathcal{L}_{adv}$ on SCUFO, and $\mathcal{L}_{gp}$ alone barely helps (29.24\%). Adding $\mathcal{L}_{ent}$ to $\mathcal{L}_{adv}$ prevents the trivial solution and improves accuracy (46.72\%); adding $\mathcal{L}_{gp}$ on top of all components gives the best and most stable result, 53.22\%. Combinations without $\mathcal{L}_{adv}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{ent}$ together are consistently worse (e.g.\ 41.11\% for $\mathcal{L}_{ent}+\mathcal{L}_{gp}$).

