\section{Limitations}
Our evaluation covers scene- and appearance-induced static biases; broader demographic and real-world fairness dimensions remain to be studied. \approachname{} assumes static information is uniformly unhelpful, which may be too strict for actions where static cues are legitimately discriminative, and the IID accuracy drop on HMDB51 reflects this trade-off. Future work could selectively retain beneficial spatial cues while suppressing spurious ones, and choose the static frame with learned indicators such as action saliency rather than fixed rules. Results are reported without repeated runs or confidence intervals, so small differences (e.g.\ between middle and random frames) should not be over-interpreted.

\section{Conclusion}
We presented \approachname, a label-free, single-encoder adversarial approach that breaks the correlation between labels and static cues by combining a negative cross-entropy, entropy maximization and a gradient penalty on static clips. Together with a corrected UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol that removes background leakage, it substantially improves robustness to both background and foreground bias, e.g.\ 53.22\% contrasted accuracy on HMDB51 versus 40.28\% for the prior state of the art, and benefits downstream tasks.

